35,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"The logs show eleven misrouted deliveries, all from duplicate address records. That is a data problem, not a people problem.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,May,,My findings are worse. The coordination group is running on two people who are already drowning.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,I found the real killer. Half the customer plants lose network coverage for whole afternoons.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The capital line is frozen, but the software allowance has room. We must pick something that fits there.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Kenji,,I found the pattern. The last three rollouts that needed daily entry from everyone were quietly dropped.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Kenji That matches what I saw. The copying waste is real, but the address duplication is the root cause.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,May,,"@Shunichi If the network is down, the data entry fails. We cannot fix the data if the signal is gone.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,@May Exactly. And the vendor pricing is a trap. Per-head licences climb with every department added.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,@Shunichi That is why I checked the standardization record. Four of six efforts died because no one owned them.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Kenji,,@Daisuke So the narrow tools survived. The broad ones died. We need a narrow tool.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I am leaning toward the address fix. It is small, but it stops the bleeding immediately.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,May,,"I am worried about the network fix. It needs developers, and they are locked up until Q3.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"The network fix is a vendor problem, not a developer problem. Fixed-fee work is cheaper and faster.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"The address fix fits the software allowance. The network fix needs capital, which is frozen.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Kenji,,The address fix is narrow. It does not need daily entry from everyone. It just needs one person to clean the data.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,I agree. The address fix is the only one that fits the budget and the staffing reality.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,May,,I can live with it. It does not add work to the coordination group. It just removes a source of errors.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,I am in. It avoids the vendor pricing trap and the network dependency. It is the safe choice.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I am in. It uses the software allowance, which is the only line with room. It is the rational choice.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I am in. It is narrow, it is specific, and it does not repeat the mistake of the last three rollouts.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
